---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import BrandHandoff from '../components/BrandHandoff.astro';
import { breadcrumbSchema, faqSchema, serviceSchema, SITE_URL } from '../utils/schema';

const url = `${SITE_URL}/strategic-operations-partner`;

const faqs = [
  {
    question: 'What is a strategic operations partner?',
    answer:
      'A strategic operations partner is a senior operator who works inside your business part-time to fix how it runs — the handoffs, the systems, the reporting, the things that break when you grow. Unlike a consultant who delivers a report, a partner stays to build and run what they recommend. Think of it as a fractional COO who also does the implementation.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How is that different from a management consultant?',
    answer:
      'A consultant diagnoses and hands you a deck. A partner diagnoses, then builds the system, trains your team on it, and is still there in month six when reality disagrees with the plan. The deliverable is a business that runs differently, not a document describing one that would.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How is it different from hiring an operations manager?',
    answer:
      'An operations manager runs the operation you already have. A strategic operations partner changes what the operation is. You usually want the partner first — otherwise you hire a manager into a mess and ask them to fix the thing they are also drowning in.',
  },
  {
    question: 'When does a business actually need one?',
    answer:
      'Usually somewhere past $500K in revenue, when the owner has become the bottleneck. The signals are consistent: everything routes through you, growth makes things worse instead of better, your team asks questions only you can answer, and the number you manage by lives in a spreadsheet somebody updates by hand.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What does a strategic operations partner cost?',
    answer:
      'Far less than the equivalent full-time hire, because it is part-time and scoped to a specific bottleneck. I work on monthly retainers and on two-week sprints for businesses that want to test the fit before committing. Details are on the services page.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How long does an engagement last?',
    answer:
      'Long enough for the systems to outlive the engagement, which is usually a few months rather than a few weeks. The goal is explicitly to become unnecessary — if you still need me in year three to keep things running, I built the wrong thing.',
  },
];

const jsonLd = [
  breadcrumbSchema([
    { name: 'Home', url: SITE_URL },
    { name: 'Strategic Operations Partner', url },
  ]),
  serviceSchema({
    name: 'Strategic Operations Partner',
    description:
      'Part-time senior operations leadership for service businesses — diagnosing bottlenecks, building systems, and staying to run them.',
    url,
  }),
  faqSchema(faqs),
];
---

<Layout
  title="What Is a Strategic Operations Partner?"
  description="A senior operator who works inside your business part-time to fix how it runs — and stays to build what they recommend. What it is, how it differs from consulting, and when you need one."
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <article class="py-16 lg:py-24">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">

      <header>
        <p class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-indigo-600 dark:text-indigo-400">Strategic Operations Partner</p>
        <h1 class="mt-3 text-4xl sm:text-5xl font-bold tracking-tight text-gray-900 dark:text-white">
          What is a strategic operations partner?
        </h1>
        <p class="mt-6 text-xl leading-relaxed text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300">
          A strategic operations partner is a senior operator who works inside your business part-time to fix how it
          runs — the handoffs, the systems, the reporting, the things that quietly break when you grow. Unlike a
          consultant who delivers a report and leaves, a partner stays to build and run what they recommend.
        </p>
      </header>

      <div class="mt-12 space-y-12">

        <section>
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 dark:text-white">Why the distinction matters</h2>
          <p class="mt-4 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
            I spent 12 years at Google in operations, programs, and product launches. The thing that transfers is not
            the scale — it is knowing what a system looks like before it exists, and being willing to build it rather
            than describe it. Most operational advice fails at the implementation step, because the person giving it
            has already left by then.
          </p>
          <p class="mt-4 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
            So the model here is simple: diagnose the bottleneck, design the system, deploy it, then build the
            discipline that keeps it running after I stop showing up.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 dark:text-white">When does a business need one?</h2>
          <p class="mt-4 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
            Usually somewhere past $500K in revenue, when the owner has become the bottleneck. The signals are
            consistent enough that most owners recognise all four:
          </p>
          <ul class="mt-6 space-y-4">
            {[
              ['Everything routes through you', 'Decisions wait for you. Quotes wait for you. The team is capable and still blocked, because the knowledge lives in your head rather than in a system.'],
              ['Growth makes things worse', 'A good month creates more chaos than a slow one. That is the clearest sign the operation does not scale — success is currently a problem.'],
              ['The real numbers live in a spreadsheet', 'You have software, and you still manage by a sheet somebody updates by hand. That gap is where money leaks.'],
              ['You have hired to fix it and it did not', 'Adding people to an operation that does not work produces a bigger operation that does not work.'],
            ].map(([t, d]) => (
              <li class="flex gap-4">
                <span class="mt-2 h-2 w-2 flex-shrink-0 rounded-full bg-indigo-600 dark:bg-indigo-400"></span>
                <div>
                  <p class="font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white">{t}</p>
                  <p class="mt-1 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300">{d}</p>
                </div>
              </li>
            ))}
          </ul>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 dark:text-white">Partner, consultant, or operations manager?</h2>
          <p class="mt-4 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
            These get used interchangeably and they are genuinely different jobs.
          </p>
          <div class="mt-6 overflow-x-auto">
            <table class="w-full text-left text-sm">
              <thead>
                <tr class="border-b-2 border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700">
                  <th class="py-3 pr-4 font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white">Role</th>
                  <th class="py-3 px-4 font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white">Delivers</th>
                  <th class="py-3 pl-4 font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white">Best when</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody class="text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300">
                <tr class="border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700">
                  <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-medium">Management consultant</td>
                  <td class="py-3 px-4">Analysis and recommendations</td>
                  <td class="py-3 pl-4">You know you have a problem but not what it is</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700">
                  <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-medium">Operations manager</td>
                  <td class="py-3 px-4">Day-to-day execution</td>
                  <td class="py-3 pl-4">The operation works and needs running</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-medium">Strategic operations partner</td>
                  <td class="py-3 px-4">Systems built, deployed, and handed over</td>
                  <td class="py-3 pl-4">The operation itself needs to change</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <p class="mt-6 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
            The sequencing matters more than the labels. Hire an operations manager into a broken operation and you
            have hired someone to drown in it. Fix the operation first, then hire someone to run it.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 dark:text-white">Common questions</h2>
          <div class="mt-6 space-y-4">
            {faqs.map((faq) => (
              <details class="group rounded-lg border border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700 p-5">
                <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white list-none flex justify-between gap-4">
                  <span>{faq.question}</span>
                  <span class="text-gray-400 transition-transform group-open:rotate-180">&#9662;</span>
                </summary>
                <p class="mt-3 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">{faq.answer}</p>
              </details>
            ))}
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="rounded-2xl bg-gray-50 dark:bg-gray-800 p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 dark:text-white">Where to go next</h2>
          <p class="mt-4 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
            If the bottleneck is how the business runs, start with
            <a href="/operations-consulting" class="text-indigo-600 dark:text-indigo-400 font-semibold">operations consulting</a>.
            If it is specifically the manual, repetitive work eating your team's week, look at
            <a href="/ai-automation-consulting" class="text-indigo-600 dark:text-indigo-400 font-semibold">AI and automation consulting</a>.
            And if what you actually need is software built rather than a process fixed, that is
            <a href="https://buildmysystem.co" class="text-indigo-600 dark:text-indigo-400 font-semibold">Build My System</a>.
          </p>
          <a href="/contact" class="mt-6 inline-block rounded-lg bg-indigo-600 px-6 py-3 font-semibold text-white hover:bg-indigo-700">
            Book a free Systems Audit call
          </a>
        </section>

      </div>
    </div>
  </article>

  <BrandHandoff variant="default" />
</Layout>
